s favoring a July hike and July FOMC minutes (released August 19) showing \"several\" officials supporting tighter policy and \"many\" viewing hikes as likely necessary if inflation fails to decline toward 2%. Persistent price pressures from energy supply shocks, Middle East tensions, and AI-driven spending have kept core PCE above target, while mixed July employment data (job losses alongside a 4.1% unemployment rate) tempers urgency. Markets price roughly 45% odds of a September 16 hike, with futures implying one tightening by year-end versus economist forecasts favoring a hold. Key near-term catalysts include August CPI/PCE releases and the September FOMC decision, where incoming data will determine whether the committee shifts from hold to action.","context_requires_regen":false,"context_updated_at":"2026-08-23T22:31:37.047Z"},"version":"v1"}]
